{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"research_plan (by Lead Analyst):
{
 "spines": [
  {
   "spine": "Public equity",
   "must_answer": "Is the market mispricing Tesla's future cash flows or its risk — i.e. does the current share price already pay for autonomy and energy growth that the filings do not yet show?"
  },
  {
   "spine": "Industrial / hardware",
   "must_answer": "Can Tesla still produce and sell vehicles at scale at a cost and margin that funds the autonomy and energy build-out?"
  },
  {
   "spine": "Policy / geopolitics-sensitive",
   "must_answer": "Does policy (US EV credits and their repeal, tariffs, EU CO2 rules, China NEV tax treatment, autonomy permitting) create a durable moat or a temporary subsidy that is now reversing?"
  }
 ],
 "industry_map": "Value chain: upstream (lithium, nickel, graphite, rare earths, cells from CATL/Panasonic/LG) → cell and pack manufacture (Tesla 4680 in-house plus purchased cells) → vehicle assembly (Fremont, Shanghai, Berlin, Texas, Nevada, Mexico) → direct distribution and service network → charging (Supercharger, NACS standard now licensed to rivals) → software (FSD supervised/unsupervised, OTA, insurance) → downstream energy (Megapack/Powerwall, grid services) → adjacent optionality (Optimus humanoid robot, AI compute/Dojo). Decisive bottleneck: cost per kWh and per vehicle at scale in the core auto business, and — for the valuation premium — regulatory approval plus safety evidence for unsupervised autonomy. Tesla's place: integrated OEM and the only Western automaker with in-house cell, software, charging and energy-storage capability at scale, but now a share loser in China and Europe rather than the price setter.",
 "geo_lens": "Material. Tesla's demand, cost base and policy exposure differ by market system, so map four jurisdictions separately: (1) United States — home market, direct-sales model, IRA/EV credit regime, NHTSA/state autonomous-vehicle permits; (2) China — Shanghai plant output, domestic share loss to BYD/Xiaomi, NEV purchase-tax and trade-in policy; (3) European Union — Berlin plant, CO2 fleet targets, tariffs on China-built EVs; (4) export/rest-of-world — Korea, Japan, Australia, Canada and tariff-driven sourcing shifts. Report revenue, deliveries and gross margin by region, not a global average.",
 "policy_lens": "Material and probably the largest single swing factor over 12 months. Map: US federal EV tax-credit regime and its repeal or phase-out effects on Tesla demand and on Tesla's regulatory-credit sales to other OEMs; NHTSA and state-level autonomous-vehicle regulation (Texas, California DMV/CPUC) that gates robotaxi revenue; EU CO2 fleet targets and the 2025-2027 flexibilities; EU and US tariffs on China-built EVs and Tesla's sourcing response; China's NEV purchase-tax preference schedule and any change in 2026; export controls on AI compute that could constrain FSD training. Distinguish durable policy openings from temporary subsidies.",
 "regulatory_databases": [
  "SEC EDGAR (10-K, 10-Q, 8-K, DEF 14A, Forms 4 and 144)",
  "NHTSA (recalls, ODI investigations, Standing General Order crash reports)",
  "California DMV and CPUC (autonomous vehicle testing and deployment permits)",
  "Texas DMV / SB 2809 autonomous vehicle permitting",
  "EPA fuel-economy and GHG data",
  "EU type-approval (KBA, RDW) and ACEA registrations",
  "China MIIT product catalogue and CPCA sales data",
  "EU Battery Regulation and CBAM filings",
  "USPTO / EPO / WIPO patent registers"
 ],
 "gates": [
  "Confirm company identity, legal entity, geography, stage, and business model.",
  "Choose 1-3 report spines from report_strategy_playbook.md.",
  "Decide whether the geographic market context lens is material; if yes, map home, revenue, expansion, supplier, comparable-company, capital, exit markets and the investor perspective.",
  "Decide whether the policy and geopolitics lens is material; if yes, map jurisdictions and perspective."
 ],
 "hypothesis": "Wait/watch rather than Buy at the current price unless autonomy revenue is evidenced. The auto business is the cash engine and it is under volume and margin pressure; energy storage is the credible growth offset; robotaxi and Optimus are options whose value must be separated from the price. The decision hinges on how much of the current market capitalization is already paid for autonomy that has not yet produced disclosed revenue.",
 "author": "Lead Analyst",
 "saved_at": "2026-10-31T06:10:11+00:00"
}"}],"isError":false}

