{"url":"https://www.fool.com/earnings/call-transcripts/2026/10/22/tesla-tsla-q3-2026-earnings-call-transcript/","title":"Tesla (TSLA) Q3 2026 Earnings Call Transcript","text":"Image source: The Motley Fool.
DATEWed, Oct. 21, 2026CALL PARTICIPANTSHead of Investor Relations - Travis AxelrodElon MuskVaibhav TanejaAshok ElluswamyLars MoravyKonstantinos LaskarisTAKEAWAYSTotal Revenue -- $28.0 billion, representing a flat performance compared to the prior year.Non-GAAP EPS -- $0.48 per diluted share, reflecting a decrease from $0.50 in the third quarter of 2025.Total Vehicle Deliveries -- 486,532 units, driven by demand for Model 3 and Model Y despite a 2% year-over-year decline in total volume.Automotive Gross Margin -- 18.1% excluding regulatory credits, representing a sequential improvement from 16.3% due to a higher delivery mix in the Asia-Pacific region.FSD Attach Rate -- Over 75% for new vehicle deliveries in North America, representing a 20 percentage point sequential increase.Energy Storage Deployments -- 13.7 GWh, with gross margins rising to 33.1% due to tariff refunds and cell-related warranty cost reimbursements.Free Cash Flow -- $1.53 billion, supported by operating cash flow of $6.26 billion.Capital Expenditures -- Approximately $20 billion for the current year, focused on Super Intelligence compute infrastructure and manufacturing capacity for new product lines.Robotaxi Mileage -- Cumulative unsupervised miles grew eightfold quarter over quarter, fueled by weekly double-digit growth in the Austin network.Robotaxi Service Launch -- Commenced Cybercab operations in Austin on Sept. 3, marking the initial deployment into the company-owned fleet.Optimus Production Rate -- Target of 20,000 units per week within approximately one year as the high-volume production line in Fremont ramps up.Megapack Backlog -- Inventory is nearly sold out through 2027, driven by high demand from global data center infrastructure.Services and Other Margin -- Reached an all-time high of 15.9%, reflecting strength in Supercharging and vehicle insurance offerings.Borrowing Capacity -- Secured up to $30 billion in new financing facilities to support the expected growth in multiyear capital expenditures.Vehicle Production Goal -- Target of 5 million vehicles annually by approximately 2030, leveraging existing capacity before building new factories.Cybercab Capacity -- Installed annual manufacturing capacity of over 125,000 units at the Texas facility.Model 3/Y Production -- 457,387 units, representing 5% growth year over year.SI Training Capacity -- Projected to reach 400 MW of compute by the end of the year to support vehicle and humanoid robot software development.Digital Asset Gains -- Recorded a $288 million mark-to-market gain on Bitcoin holdings.SpaceX Investment Impact -- Recognized a $142 million mark-to-market reduction in the value of equity holdings.Regulatory Credit Revenue -- $113 million, representing a decrease from $417 million in the prior-year period.Inventory Days of Supply -- Ended the quarter at 11 days, reflecting efficient management of global vehicle inventory levels.Need a quote from a Motley Fool analyst? Email [email protected]RISKSTaneja stated, "commodity price increases and interest rate changes continue to add to our cost and create further headwinds," noting potential impacts on automotive margins.Musk warned that the initial production ramp for Optimus is expected to have a "shallow slope and is somewhat bumpy" because of the complexity of the hardware.Taneja noted that vehicle deliveries were "constrained primarily by production" in the quarter as factories work to reach maximum utilization.SUMMARYTesla, Inc. (TSLA -0.86%) reported third quarter results that highlighted a strategic transition toward autonomy, robotics, and expanded energy storage deployments. Management reported that demand for electric vehicles currently exceeds production capacity, with Full Self-Driving subscriptions serving as a key driver for new customer acquisition. The company stated that its energy storage division is experiencing significant demand from data centers, contributing to record backlogs and improved segment margins. Significant capital investments are underway to develop domestic battery manufacturing and semiconductor production capabilities to support long-term growth targets. Management indicated that the company is focused on maximizing factory utilization while scaling the newly launched Robotaxi service and humanoid robot production.Musk stated that the company expects Full Self-Driving approval in additional markets, including a potential EU-wide approval by the first quarter of 2027.Management reported that the Austin-based Terafab research facility will begin commissioning machinery in the first half of 2027 with initial test chips expected by June 2027.Musk indicated that the integration of the AI5 chip into Optimus robots, planned for approximately April 2027, will provide an order of magnitude increase in compute capability.Elluswamy noted that the company is developing a proprietary AI-based mapping and navigation architecture to replace current dependence on third-party maps.Musk stated that the Cybercab will be primarily utilized as transport-as-a-service through a Tesla-owned fleet rather than being prioritized for individual ownership.Management confirmed that Robotaxi services will expand to Dallas next month, utilizing the same software platform deployed in Austin.INDUSTRY GLOSSARYSI: Super Intelligence, Tesla's advanced artificial intelligence and high-performance computing infrastructure.FSD: Full Self-Driving, Tesla's advanced driver-assistance software requiring active driver supervision.Terafab: A dedicated semiconductor manufacturing facility being developed by Tesla to address internal supply chain needs.Optimus: Tesla's general-purpose humanoid robot currently in early production phases.Megapack: A large-scale lithium-ion battery energy storage system designed for commercial and utility applications.LFP: Lithium Iron Phosphate, a battery chemistry used in energy storage and standard-range vehicle models.Cybercab: A purpose-built autonomous vehicle designed for the company's Robotaxi network.AI5: Tesla's next-generation artificial intelligence hardware chip designed for high-compute autonomous tasks.Cortex: The primary high-performance AI training compute cluster located at Tesla's Texas headquarters.v15: The anticipated 15th major iteration of Tesla's Full Self-Driving software architecture.Full Conference Call TranscriptTravis Axelrod: Good afternoon, everyone, and welcome to Tesla's Third Quarter 2026 Q&A Webcast. My name is Travis Axelrod, Head of Investor Relations, and I'm joined today by Elon Musk, Vaibhav Taneja and a number of other executives. Our Q3 results were announced at about 3:00 p.m. Central Time in the update deck we published at the same link as this webcast. During this call, we will discuss our business outlook and make forward-looking statements. These comments are based on our predictions and expectations as of today.Actual events or results could differ materially due to a number of risks and uncertainties, including those mentioned in our most recent filings with the SEC. [Operator Instructions] Before we jump into Q&A, Elon has some opening remarks. Elon?Elon Musk: Thank you. So the third quarter was very strong, and I'd like to give a big congratulations to the whole Tesla team for working hard to make it happen. Demand is growing rapidly for our vehicles. It's exceeding our production by a significant margin. And obviously, we're going to be ramping and we are ramping production meaningfully. The biggest driver for demand is full self-driving. And today, there's really no competitor for FSD in any car that you can buy. And to be clear, we are producing and delivering as many cars as we can, and we're pushing very hard for FSD approval in additional markets.We're now approved in many countries in Europe, and we're hoping for an EU-wide approval. It's hard to predict, but maybe within a few months, but I would be surprised if it takes longer than Q1. But obviously, tha

