{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"red_team_review (by Red Team Analyst):
{
 "answers": {
  "most_damaging_fact": "The 'no buyer can pay' claim. SpaceX is listed at $2.01tn ($147.79 on 2026-10-20), both boards have independent special committees, and RBC and JPMorgan expect a 20-35% premium, $480-540 against $399.81 [S154, S156]. At the report's 15% merger weight that branch moves the stock +26.6% to $506.05, so the Sell is wrong by 20-35% if terms land inside twelve months. The second candidate is the 2.0m unsupervised-mile figure: if the 8x quarterly growth is independently verified and a California deployment permit follows, valuing autonomy at zero is wrong. The DMV list on 2026-10-23 still shows testing with a safety driver [S89], which is why the Sell survives today.",
  "company_only_claims": "The positive case rests on company-only numbers: 2.0m cumulative unsupervised Robotaxi miles growing 8x quarter on quarter, the 1,051-vehicle Texas fleet, 'almost sold out' Megapacks for 2027, 1.74m FSD subscriptions including upfront buyers and the $1bn FSD run rate [S152]. The energy margin is no longer a positive company claim: Q3 2026's 33.1% is $526m of tariff refunds and warranty true-ups; ex-refund it is $428m on $2,881m, 14.8%, barely above Fluence's FY2025 13.1% [S174] and far from its 5.1% June quarter [S61]. Independent checks are the California DMV list, which shows testing only, and NHTSA EA26002; both cut against the timeline [S87, S89].",
  "generic_section": "technology_product is no longer the weakest: it corrected Waymo to 220.6m rider-only miles, made Tesla 0.9% of the incumbent, and cut to six tags [S151]. market is the weakest now, but it is a disclosed gap rather than a generic section: Tesla's EU unit share is not disclosed, so Europe's 49% BEV growth is a market fact, not a Tesla share gain; the ACEA manufacturer table would settle it, and the section says so. competition is second: it still carries Waymo at '170m+' and a 'sixfold' storage margin that the 14.8% ex-refund figure contradicts [S8, S61, S174]. Both are corrections, not new research.",
  "unpriced_risk": "The robotaxi cost per paid mile. The recommendation prices the de-rating and gives the merger branch 15%, but it treats the 15% autonomy branch as a permit event: Tesla discloses no revenue, rides, fare, insurance, remote-supervision ratio or cost per mile, and if cost per paid mile exceeds fare the branch is worth zero even with a California permit, taking expected return from -25.2% to -34.2% [S153]. NHTSA's remedy ($0.3-3.2bn) is a second unpriced cost channel; the crowded-short risk runs the other way, with 2.19% of float and 2.2 days to cover a light base [S155].",
  "first_challenge": "The base case and the SOTP are 5-7x apart: $266 against $40-50, so which number is the recommendation asking me to underwrite? If the twelve-month target is $266 (-33.5%), the SOTP is a liquidation floor and the Sell is a de-rating call; if it is $40-50, the Sell is an -87% to -90% call the thesis does not defend. Second, the exit section still carries a 25-45% probability band and a -17% to -1.5% weighted return, while the report's single distribution gives the merger 15% and an expected -25.2%; one of the two has to give. Third, the 30% bull is defensible, but 15% of it is an autonomy branch whose cost per paid mile has never been published.",
  "downside_survival": "Yes, as a de-rating call. A 50% smaller market takes the SOTP to $120-126bn, $30-32 a share, and destroys the base case's 70x on $14.5bn; revenue 30% lower gives $90 a share at 40x; CAC 2x adds $3.7bn and wipes the $3.47bn operating income; a six-month financing delay is not a solvency event with $36.18bn of net cash and $30.0bn undrawn, so the cost falls on the option. Multiples compress is the base and bear; free cash flow 30% lower changes little at a 0.2% yield. The conclusion fails only if the merger branch lands or the autonomy cost stack is positive, so the 15% weights, the 2.9% NAV cap and the $506.05 stop are the weak points, not the downside arithmetic.",
  "ocr_claims": "No OCR-derived claims. Financial figures come from the 10-Q and the Q3 2026 update, governance from the proxy, permits from the California DMV page, all text-readable [S8, S10, S89]. The NHTSA EA26002 PDF did not yield machine-readable text in this pass, so the investigation scope (3,203,754 vehicles, nine incidents, one fatality) rests on the filing-based technology section and the registered NHTSA source rather than a fresh OCR reading [S87]; that is a corroboration gap, not an OCR claim."
 },
 "weakest_section": "market",
